(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Computer Organization"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Computer Organization"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Computer Organization: Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nUse RISC-V RV32I and the standard five-stage pipeline unless told otherwise. Hex values of instruction encodings, program results, instruction counts, cache traces and branch-predictor results below were produced by running an assembler, an emulator and cache and predictor simulators. Show your working: marks are given for method and for correct units.\n\n# Assignment 1: Performance, Data Representation and the Instruction Set (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Performance (10 marks)** (a) A program mix has ALU 50 percent (CPI 1), loads 20 percent (CPI 2), stores 10 percent (CPI 2) and branches 20 percent (CPI 3). Find the average CPI, and the execution time and MIPS rate for 10^9 instructions at 2 GHz [4]. (b) Machine A (3 GHz, CPI 2.0) and machine B (2 GHz, CPI 1.2) run the same program. Which is faster and by how much? [3] (c) A component used 40 percent of the time is made 10 times faster: find the speedup (Amdahl) [2]. (d) Reducing both the supply voltage and the clock frequency by 15 percent changes the dynamic power by what factor? [1]\n\n**Q2. Data representation (10 marks)** (a) Show the bytes in memory order for 0x12345678 stored in little-endian and big-endian format [2]. (b) Encode 13.25 and -0.1 as IEEE 754 single-precision numbers (hex) [4]. (c) Decode the single-precision pattern 0xC2ED4000 [2]. (d) Compute 1.5 + 2.25 in single precision and give the hex of the result [2].\n\n**Q3. Instruction encoding (12 marks)** (a) Encode as hex: add x5, x6, x7; addi x5, x6, -1; lw x10, 8(x2); sw x10, 12(x2) [6]. (b) Decode 0x40A48433 and 0xFFF30293 [4]. (c) State how many bits the branch offset field of beq has and the range of the branch [2].\n\n**Q4. Reading assembly (14 marks)** (a) Give the final values of x7 to x14 after the following (x5 = 12, x6 = 5 initially) [6]:\n```\nadd x7, x5, x6\nsub x8, x5, x6\nand x9, x5, x6\nor  x10, x5, x6\nxor x11, x5, x6\nslli x12, x5, 3\nsrli x13, x5, 2\nslt x14, x6, x5\n```\n(b) The array {3, 5, 7, 9} is at address 256. Explain the program in Section 2.4 of the notes (the array sum), give the final value of x5 and the number of instructions executed [5]. (c) What does the following compute for x5 = 48 and x6 = 18? [3]\n```\nloop: beq  x5, x6, done\n      blt  x5, x6, less\n      sub  x5, x5, x6\n      j    loop\nless: sub  x6, x6, x5\n      j    loop\ndone:\n```\n\n**Q5. Writing assembly (14 marks)** (a) Write a program that finds the maximum of the four-word array at address 256 and leaves it in x5 (test with {4, 9, 2, 7}); give the number of instructions executed [8]. (b) Write a procedure `dbl` that doubles its argument a0 and returns, and a caller that calls it with 5 [3]. (c) Load the constants 0x12345678 and 0x12345FFF into registers using lui and addi [3].\n\n**Q6. Procedures and the stack (10 marks)** (a) Explain the roles of ra, sp and the saved registers s0 to s11 in a procedure call [3]. (b) Write the prologue and epilogue of a procedure that calls another procedure and uses s0 [4]. (c) Why does recursion need a stack frame per activation? [3]\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: CPI = Σ (fraction × CPI) = 0.5(1) + 0.2(2) + 0.1(2) + 0.2(3) = 0.5 + 0.4 + 0.2 + 0.6 = **1.7**.\nStep 2: time = IC × CPI/f = 10^9 × 1.7/(2 × 10^9) = **0.85 s**.\nStep 3: MIPS = f/(CPI × 10^6) = 2000/1.7 = **1176 MIPS** (check: 10^9 instructions/0.85 s = 1.176 × 10^9 per second).\n(b) Step 1: time per instruction on A = CPI/f = 2.0/(3 × 10^9) = 0.667 ns.\nStep 2: on B = 1.2/(2 × 10^9) = 0.6 ns.\nStep 3: ratio = 0.667/0.6 = 1.11: **B is faster by 1.11 times** (11 percent), even though its clock is slower, because of its lower CPI.\n(c) Amdahl: speedup = 1/((1 - f) + f/s) = 1/((1 - 0.4) + 0.4/10) = 1/(0.6 + 0.04) = 1/0.64 = **1.5625**.\n(d) P ∝ V²f, so the factor is 0.85² × 0.85 = 0.7225 × 0.85 = 0.85³ = **0.614**: a 39 percent reduction in dynamic power for a 15 percent frequency loss.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Little-endian: the least significant byte at the lowest address: **78 56 34 12** at increasing addresses. Big-endian: **12 34 56 78**.\n(b) **13.25:**\nStep 1: 13 = 1101₂ and 0.25 = 0.01₂, so 13.25 = 1101.01₂ = 1.10101 × 2³.\nStep 2: sign 0; exponent = 3 + 127 = 130 = 10000010₂; fraction 10101 followed by zeros.\nStep 3: bits 0 10000010 10101000000000000000000 = 0100 0001 0101 0100 0... = **0x41540000**.\n**-0.1:**\nStep 1: 0.1 = 1.100110011...₂ × 2⁻⁴ (0.1 × 16 = 1.6 and 0.6 = 0.1001 1001...₂).\nStep 2: sign 1; exponent = -4 + 127 = 123 = 01111011₂; fraction 10011001100110011001100 followed by 1100..., rounded to 10011001100110011001101.\nStep 3: bits 1 01111011 10011001100110011001101 = **0xBDCCCCCD**.\n(c) 0xC2ED4000:\nStep 1: binary 1100 0010 1110 1101 0100 0000 ... = 1 | 10000101 | 11011010100000000000000.\nStep 2: sign negative; exponent = 133 - 127 = 6.\nStep 3: significand = 1.1101101010₂ = 1 + 0.5 + 0.25 + 0.0625 + 0.03125 + 0.0078125 + 0.001953125 = 1.853515625.\nStep 4: value = -1.853515625 × 2⁶ = -1.853515625 × 64 = **-118.625**.\n(d) Step 1: 1.5 = 1.1₂ × 2⁰ = 0x3FC00000; 2.25 = 1.001₂ × 2¹ = 0x40100000.\nStep 2: the sum is 1.5 + 2.25 = 3.75 = 11.11₂ = 1.111₂ × 2¹.\nStep 3: exponent = 1 + 127 = 128 = 10000000₂; fraction 111 followed by zeros: 0 10000000 11100... = **0x40700000** (exact).\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Field layout R-type: funct7 | rs2 | rs1 | funct3 | rd | opcode.\nStep 1: add x5, x6, x7: funct7 0000000, rs2 = 7, rs1 = 6, funct3 000, rd = 5, opcode 0110011 → **0x007302B3**.\nStep 2: addi x5, x6, -1: imm = 111111111111 (0xFFF), rs1 = 6, funct3 000, rd = 5, opcode 0010011 → **0xFFF30293**.\nStep 3: lw x10, 8(x2): imm = 8, rs1 = 2, funct3 010, rd = 10, opcode 0000011 → **0x00812503**.\nStep 4: sw x10, 12(x2): imm[11:5] = 0, rs2 = 10, rs1 = 2, funct3 010, imm[4:0] = 12, opcode 0100011 → **0x00A12623**.\n(b) Step 1: 0x40A48433: opcode = 0x33 (R-type); funct7 = 0x20 (0100000), rs2 = 10, rs1 = 9, funct3 = 0, rd = 8: with funct7 0x20 and funct3 0 the operation is subtraction: **sub x8, x9, x10**.\nStep 2: 0xFFF30293: opcode 0x13 (I-type arithmetic), rd = 5, funct3 = 0, rs1 = 6, imm = 0xFFF = -1: **addi x5, x6, -1**.\n(c) The branch offset is a **13-bit signed value** (bit 0 is always 0, so 12 bits are stored): the range is -2¹² to 2¹² - 2 = **-4096 to +4094 bytes** relative to the branch (about ±2¹¹ instructions).\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) x5 = 12 = 1100₂, x6 = 5 = 0101₂.\nStep 1: x7 = 12 + 5 = **17**; x8 = 12 - 5 = **7**.\nStep 2: x9 = 1100 AND 0101 = 0100 = **4**; x10 = 1100 OR 0101 = 1101 = **13**; x11 = 1100 XOR 0101 = 1001 = **9**.\nStep 3: x12 = 12 << 3 = 12 × 8 = **96**; x13 = 12 >> 2 = 12/4 = **3**; x14 = (5 < 12) = **1**.\nSo x7 to x14 = 17, 7, 4, 13, 9, 96, 3, 1.\n(b) Step 1: the program keeps the sum in x5, the index i in x6, n = 4 in x7 and the base address in x8. Each iteration computes the byte offset 4i (slli by 2), adds the base, loads a[i], adds it to the sum and increments i; the loop ends when i ≥ n.\nStep 2: sum = 3 + 5 + 7 + 9 = **24** (final x5).\nStep 3: instructions executed: 4 (setup) + 4 iterations × 7 (bge, slli, add, lw, add, addi, j) = 28 + 1 (the final bge that exits) + 1 (the final nop at `done`) = 4 + 28 + 1 + 1 = **34**.\n(c) This is **subtractive Euclid**: it repeatedly subtracts the smaller number from the larger until they are equal.\nStep 1: (x5, x6) = (48, 18): 48 > 18, so x5 = 48 - 18 = 30.\nStep 2: (30, 18): x5 = 12.\nStep 3: (12, 18): 12 < 18, so x6 = 18 - 12 = 6.\nStep 4: (12, 6): x5 = 6.\nStep 5: (6, 6): equal, so exit with **x5 = 6 = gcd(48, 18)**.\n\n**Q5.**\n(a)\n```\n      li   x8, 256         # base\n      lw   x5, 0(x8)       # max = a[0]\n      li   x6, 1           # i = 1\n      li   x7, 4           # n\nloop: bge  x6, x7, done\n      slli x9, x6, 2\n      add  x9, x9, x8\n      lw   x10, 0(x9)      # a[i]\n      bge  x5, x10, skip   # if max >= a[i] skip\n      mv   x5, x10         # max = a[i]\nskip: addi x6, x6, 1\n      j    loop\ndone:\n```\nTrace for {4, 9, 2, 7}: x5 = 4 at the start.\nStep 1: i = 1: a[1] = 9; 4 ≥ 9 is false, so mv executes: x5 = 9 (8 instructions: bge, slli, add, lw, bge, mv, addi, j).\nStep 2: i = 2: a[2] = 2; 9 ≥ 2, skip (7 instructions).\nStep 3: i = 3: a[3] = 7; 9 ≥ 7, skip (7 instructions).\nStep 4: i = 4: the bge exits (1 instruction).\nResult **x5 = 9**. Count: 4 (setup and first load) + 8 + 7 + 7 + 1 + 1 (final nop) = **28 instructions**.\n(b)\n```\n      li   a0, 5\n      jal  ra, dbl         # ra = address of the next instruction\n      mv   a1, a0          # a1 = 10\n      j    end\ndbl:  slli a0, a0, 1       # a0 = 2 * a0\n      ret                  # jalr x0, 0(ra)\nend:\n```\nThe result is **a0 = 5 × 2 = 10**.\n(c) For 0x12345678: `lui x5, 0x12345` then `addi x5, x5, 0x678` (0x12345000 + 0x678 = 0x12345678).\nFor 0x12345FFF: Step 1: the low 12 bits 0xFFF would be sign-extended by addi to -1 (0xFFFFFFFF), so the upper part must be incremented to compensate. Step 2: `lui x5, 0x12346` then `addi x5, x5, -1`: 0x12346000 - 1 = **0x12345FFF**.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) **ra** holds the return address written by jal (and read by ret). **sp** points to the top of the stack in memory. The **saved registers s0 to s11** must have the same value when the callee returns as when it was called: a callee that wants to use one must save it on the stack first and restore it before returning. (The caller must save any a and t registers it needs after the call.)\n(b)\n```\nfunc: addi sp, sp, -16     # allocate a frame\n      sw   ra, 12(sp)      # save the return address (because we call another procedure)\n      sw   s0, 8(sp)       # save s0 (because we use it)\n      ...                  # body (may call other procedures)\n      lw   s0, 8(sp)       # restore s0\n      lw   ra, 12(sp)      # restore ra\n      addi sp, sp, 16      # free the frame\n      ret\n```\nSteps: allocate, save in order, run the body, restore in the reverse order, free the frame, return; the frame is 16 bytes to keep the stack 16-byte aligned.\n(c) Each recursive activation has its own arguments, local variables, saved registers and return address. These must exist simultaneously (the earlier calls are still waiting for results), so each call gets a new frame on the stack; the stack depth equals the recursion depth, which is why deep recursion can overflow the stack.\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 2: The Processor and Pipelining (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Single-cycle timing (10 marks)** Component delays: instruction fetch 200 ps, register read 100 ps, ALU 200 ps, data memory 200 ps, register write 100 ps. (a) Find the latency of lw, sw, R-type and beq in a single-cycle design [4]. (b) Find the minimum clock period and explain the inefficiency [2]. (c) Find the time for 10^6 instructions with this clock and with a 5-stage pipeline whose clock is set by the 200 ps stages (assume CPI 1 and ignore start-up) [4].\n\n**Q2. Control signals (10 marks)** Give the values of RegWrite, ALUSrc, MemRead, MemWrite, MemtoReg, Branch and the ALU operation for add (R-type), lw, sw and beq in a single-cycle RISC-V datapath (use X for don't-care).\n\n**Q3. Pipeline speedup (8 marks)** (a) A 5-stage pipeline with 200 ps stages executes n instructions without hazards. Give the total time and compute it for n = 1 and n = 1000 [4]. (b) Compare with the single-cycle time (800 ps per instruction) and give the speedup for n = 1000 [2]. (c) Why does the speedup not equal 5? [2]\n\n**Q4. Hazards (16 marks)** Consider `lw x1, 0(x2); add x3, x1, x4; sub x5, x3, x6; sw x5, 0(x7)`. (a) Identify all data dependences [3]. (b) Count the cycles to execute the sequence in a 5-stage pipeline (i) ideally with no hazards, (ii) with full forwarding, (iii) without forwarding (the register file can be written in the first half and read in the second half of a cycle) [6]. (c) Show how reordering helps for `lw x1, 0(x2); lw x3, 4(x2); add x4, x1, x3; sw x4, 8(x2); lw x5, 12(x2); sub x6, x5, x1; sw x6, 16(x2)` with forwarding [4]. (d) Name and explain the three kinds of hazards [3].\n\n**Q5. Branch prediction (12 marks)** A loop-closing branch has the outcome pattern T T T T N repeated three times (a loop that executes 5 iterations, three times, each loop visited in sequence). (a) Count the mispredictions of a static \"always taken\" predictor [2]. (b) Count the mispredictions of a 1-bit dynamic predictor starting in the not-taken state [3]. (c) Count the mispredictions of a 2-bit saturating counter (states 0 and 1 predict not taken, 2 and 3 predict taken) starting at state 1 [4]. (d) Compute the CPI of a pipeline with 20 percent branches, a 10 percent misprediction rate and a 2-cycle penalty [3].\n\n**Q6. CPI and stalls (8 marks)** In a program 25 percent of the instructions are loads and 40 percent of loads are followed immediately by an instruction using the loaded value. With forwarding, each such load-use costs one stall cycle. Find the CPI, ignoring other hazards, and the effect of a compiler that removes half of the load-use stalls.\n\n**Q7. Superscalar and limits (6 marks)** (a) A dual-issue processor sustains an IPC of 1.4 at 2 GHz. Find the execution time for 3 × 10^9 instructions [3]. (b) List two reasons why a superscalar processor rarely reaches its peak IPC [3].\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Add the stages each instruction uses:\nStep 1: lw: fetch 200 + register read 100 + ALU 200 + memory 200 + write 100 = **800 ps**.\nStep 2: sw: 200 + 100 + 200 + 200 = **700 ps**.\nStep 3: R-type: 200 + 100 + 200 + 100 = **600 ps**.\nStep 4: beq: 200 + 100 + 200 = **500 ps**.\n(b) The clock period must accommodate the slowest instruction: **800 ps**. Every instruction takes 800 ps, even those that need only 500 ps, so the faster instructions waste time.\n(c) Step 1: single-cycle: 10^6 × 800 ps = 8 × 10^8 ps = **0.8 ms**.\nStep 2: pipelined: 10^6 × 200 ps = 2 × 10^8 ps = **0.2 ms** (plus 4 cycles of start-up, which is negligible).\nStep 3: speedup = 0.8/0.2 = **4**, from the higher clock speed with CPI near 1.\n\n**Q2.**\n- add: RegWrite 1, ALUSrc 0, MemRead 0, MemWrite 0, MemtoReg 0, Branch 0, ALU op add.\n- lw: RegWrite 1, ALUSrc 1, MemRead 1, MemWrite 0, MemtoReg 1, Branch 0, ALU op add (address calculation).\n- sw: RegWrite 0, ALUSrc 1, MemRead 0, MemWrite 1, MemtoReg X, Branch 0, ALU op add.\n- beq: RegWrite 0, ALUSrc 0, MemRead 0, MemWrite 0, MemtoReg X, Branch 1, ALU op subtract (the Zero flag controls the branch).\nReasoning: ALUSrc = 1 when the second ALU input is the immediate (lw, sw); RegWrite = 1 only when a register receives a result (add, lw); MemtoReg is a don't-care when no register is written. (2 marks per instruction type with the correct pattern.)\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Step 1: the first instruction finishes after 5 cycles and one more finishes every cycle after that: cycles = 5 + (n - 1) = n + 4, time = **(n + 4) × 200 ps**.\nStep 2: n = 1: 5 × 200 = **1000 ps**.\nStep 3: n = 1000: 1004 × 200 ps = 200,800 ps = **200.8 ns**.\n(b) Step 1: single-cycle: 1000 × 800 ps = 800 ns.\nStep 2: speedup = 800/200.8 = **3.98**.\n(c) The ideal speedup of 5 needs balanced stages (800/5 = 160 ps), but the slowest stage (200 ps) sets the clock, and there are pipeline register overhead and hazards. The latency of each instruction is not reduced (5 × 200 = 1000 ps in the pipeline against 800 ps single-cycle); the **throughput** improves.\n\n**Q4.** Code: lw x1,0(x2); add x3,x1,x4; sub x5,x3,x6; sw x5,0(x7).\n(a) add reads x1 written by lw (RAW, load-use); sub reads x3 written by add (RAW); sw reads x5 written by sub (RAW, the stored data).\n(b) (i) Ideal: 5 + (4 - 1) = **8 cycles**.\n(ii) With full forwarding: the load-use dependence needs one bubble, because the loaded value exists only at the end of MEM; sub and sw get their operands by forwarding with no further stalls: 8 + 1 = **9 cycles**.\n(iii) Without forwarding: a consumer in the instruction right after its producer must wait until the producer's write-back (first half of the cycle), which costs 2 stall cycles per dependence: 3 dependences × 2 = 6 stalls, so 8 + 6 = **14 cycles**.\n(c) Sequence: lw x1; lw x3; add x4,x1,x3; sw x4; lw x5; sub x6,x5,x1; sw x6.\nStep 1: 7 instructions ideally need 5 + 6 = 11 cycles.\nStep 2: as written, add uses x3 loaded just before it (1 stall) and sub uses x5 loaded just before it (1 stall): 11 + 2 = **13 cycles**.\nStep 3: reorder to `lw x1; lw x3; lw x5; add x4,x1,x3; sub x6,x5,x1; sw x4,8(x2); sw x6,16(x2)`: each load result is used at least two instructions later, so there are **no stalls**: **11 cycles**.\n(d) **Structural hazard:** two instructions need the same resource at the same time (for example one memory for instructions and data). **Data hazard:** an instruction depends on the result of a previous instruction still in the pipeline (RAW). **Control hazard:** the next instruction to fetch is not known until a branch is resolved.\n\n**Q5.** Outcomes (15 branches): T T T T N, T T T T N, T T T T N.\n(a) \"Always taken\" is wrong once per loop exit: **3 mispredictions** (positions 5, 10, 15).\n(b) 1-bit predictor starting at not-taken (it predicts the previous outcome):\nStep 1: position 1 (T): predicted N, miss; predictor becomes T.\nStep 2: positions 2 to 4 (T): predicted T, correct.\nStep 3: position 5 (N): predicted T, miss; predictor becomes N.\nStep 4: position 6 (T): predicted N, miss; and so on: misses at 1, 5, 6, 10, 11, 15: **6 mispredictions** (2 per loop after the first).\n(c) 2-bit counter starting at state 1 (weakly not taken; states 2 and 3 predict taken):\nStep 1: position 1 (T): predict N, miss; state 2.\nStep 2: positions 2 to 4 (T): predict T, correct; state 3.\nStep 3: position 5 (N): predict T, miss; state 2.\nStep 4: position 6 (T): predict T, correct; state 3 ... the same afterwards: only the loop exit is missed (positions 10 and 15).\nTotal misses at 1, 5, 10, 15 = **4 mispredictions**. The hysteresis is the advantage of 2-bit predictors.\n(d) CPI = 1 + (branch fraction × misprediction rate × penalty) = 1 + 0.2 × 0.1 × 2 = 1 + 0.04 = **1.04**.\n\n**Q6.** Step 1: stall fraction = 0.25 × 0.40 = 0.10 stalls per instruction.\nStep 2: **CPI = 1 + 0.10 = 1.10**.\nStep 3: the compiler removes half of them: CPI = 1 + 0.05 = **1.05**.\nStep 4: speedup = 1.10/1.05 = **1.048** (4.8 percent) by scheduling alone.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) Time = IC/(IPC × f) = 3 × 10^9/(1.4 × 2 × 10^9) = 3/2.8 = **1.07 s**.\n(b) Limits on IPC: data dependences between instructions (limited instruction-level parallelism), branch mispredictions and control dependences, cache misses that stall the issue, and structural limits (the number of functional units and ports).\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 3: Memory Hierarchy, Virtual Memory, I/O and Parallelism (80 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Cache organisation (10 marks)** (a) A 16 KB direct-mapped cache with 64-byte blocks and 32-bit addresses: find the number of lines, the offset, index and tag widths, and the total bits of storage including the valid bit [5]. (b) A 32 KB 4-way set-associative cache with 64-byte blocks: find the number of sets and the field widths [3]. (c) What is the effect of doubling the block size on the fields? [2]\n\n**Q2. Cache trace (14 marks)** A cache has 8 blocks of 16 bytes. Byte addresses are accessed in the order 0, 4, 16, 132, 232, 160, 1024, 30, 140, 3100, 180, 2180. For each of (a) direct-mapped, (b) 2-way set-associative with LRU, (c) fully associative with LRU, give the hit/miss sequence and the number of hits. Explain the differences.\n\n**Q3. Locality and miss behaviour (12 marks)** (a) A loop reads a 64-byte array word by word (4-byte words) into a cold direct-mapped cache with 16-byte blocks: give the hit/miss pattern and the miss rate [3]. (b) Two arrays A and B lie 1024 bytes apart and the loop `for i: C += A[i] * B[i]` accesses A[i], B[i] alternately in a 1 KB direct-mapped cache with 16-byte blocks: show that every access misses, and show what a 2-way associative cache does [5]. (c) A 128 × 128 integer matrix (4 bytes per element, row-major) is traversed in a 16 KB direct-mapped cache with 64-byte blocks: count the misses for row-by-row and column-by-column traversal [4].\n\n**Q4. Average memory access time (8 marks)** (a) Find the AMAT for a hit time of 1 ns, a miss rate of 5 percent and a miss penalty of 100 ns [2]. (b) For a two-level hierarchy with an L1 hit time of 1 ns and a 10 percent L1 miss rate, an L2 hit time of 10 ns and a 20 percent local L2 miss rate, and a memory time of 100 ns, find the AMAT [3]. (c) A processor has a base CPI of 1 and 30 percent of instructions access data; the data cache miss rate is 4 percent and the miss penalty is 50 cycles. Find the CPI [3].\n\n**Q5. Write policies (8 marks)** Compare write-through and write-back caches: behaviour on write hits, memory traffic, consistency and complexity; state the role of the write buffer and of write-allocate.\n\n**Q6. Virtual memory (12 marks)** (a) A system has 32-bit virtual addresses and 4 KB pages: give the offset and virtual page number widths and the size of a flat page table with 4-byte entries [3]. (b) For a two-level page table with 10 bits at each level, find the page-table memory for a process that uses only 8 MB of contiguous memory [3]. (c) Describe the steps of an address translation with a TLB hit, a TLB miss and a page fault [4]. (d) If a memory access takes 200 ns and a page fault takes 8 ms, find the effective access time for a page-fault rate of 10^-6 [2].\n\n**Q7. I/O (6 marks)** A 100 MHz CPU polls a device status register. (a) If each poll takes 100 cycles and the device must be polled 1000 times per second, compute the CPU time used per second by polling [2]. (b) Compare with interrupt-driven I/O when each interrupt costs 2000 cycles and the device generates 100 events per second, and explain when DMA is preferable [4].\n\n**Q8. Parallelism (8 marks)** (a) A program is 5 percent serial. Find the speedup on 8 cores and on 1000 cores, and the limit [4]. (b) Explain cache coherence and why it is needed in a multicore processor [2]. (c) Give two examples of data-parallel computation suited to SIMD or GPUs [2].\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: lines = 16 KB/64 B = 16,384/64 = **256 lines**.\nStep 2: offset = log2 64 = **6 bits**; index = log2 256 = **8 bits**; tag = 32 - 6 - 8 = **18 bits**.\nStep 3: bits per line = 1 (valid) + 18 (tag) + 64 × 8 (data) = 1 + 18 + 512 = 531.\nStep 4: total = 256 × 531 = **135,936 bits** (16.6 KiB). Overhead = 256 × 19/(256 × 512) = 3.7 percent.\n(b) Step 1: lines = 32 KB/64 B = 512; sets = 512/4 = **128**.\nStep 2: offset 6 bits, index log2 128 = **7 bits**, tag 32 - 6 - 7 = **19 bits**.\n(c) Doubling the block size (same capacity) adds one offset bit and halves the number of lines, removing one index bit; the tag width stays the same for a direct-mapped cache. Larger blocks exploit spatial locality but raise the miss penalty and the risk of conflicts.\n\n**Q2.** Step 1: block address = address ÷ 16: 0, 0, 1, 8, 14, 10, 64, 1, 8, 193, 11, 136.\nStep 2 (direct-mapped, 8 lines): index = block mod 8: 0, 0, 1, 0, 6, 2, 0, 1, 0, 1, 3, 0.\n(a) **Direct-mapped: M H M M M M M H M M M M, 2 hits.** Trace: addr 0 miss; 4 hit (same block 0); 16 miss (block 1); 132 miss (block 8 maps to index 0 and evicts block 0); 232 miss; 160 miss; 1024 miss (block 64, index 0); 30 hit (block 1 still at index 1); 140 miss (block 8 was evicted by 1024); 3100, 180 and 2180 miss.\n(b) Step 3 (2-way, 4 sets, LRU): set = block mod 4: 0, 0, 1, 0, 2, 2, 0, 1, 0, 1, 3, 0. **M H M M M M M H H M M M, 3 hits.** The extra hit is address 140: set 0 holds blocks 8 and 64 together, so block 8 survives.\n(c) Step 4 (fully associative, 8 lines, LRU): **M H M M M M M H H M M M, 3 hits.** Only 10 distinct blocks are used, and the hits are the same as in (b): no conflict misses remain; the other misses are compulsory (first touches) or capacity.\nConclusion: associativity removes **conflict misses** (blocks 0, 8 and 64 all compete for index 0 in the direct-mapped cache); compulsory misses cannot be avoided by any organisation.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Step 1: a 16-byte block holds 16/4 = 4 words; 64 bytes = 16 words = 4 blocks.\nStep 2: the first word of each block misses and the next three hit: the pattern is **M H H H** repeated four times.\nStep 3: miss rate = 4/16 = **25 percent** (the benefit of spatial locality).\n(b) Step 1: 1 KB cache with 16-byte blocks has 64 lines; A[i] and B[i] are 1024 bytes apart = 64 blocks, so they have the same index (block mod 64).\nStep 2: access sequence A0, B0, A1, B1, ... (addresses 0, 1024, 4, 1028, ...): each access to B evicts the block that A just loaded and vice versa, so **M M M M M M M M: every access misses (conflict misses)** although the arrays have perfect spatial locality.\nStep 3: 2-way cache: both blocks fit in the same set, so **M M H H H H H H** for the first 8 accesses (a miss only on the first touch of each block).\n(c) Step 1: the matrix is 128 × 128 × 4 = 65,536 bytes = 64 KB, larger than the 16 KB cache.\nStep 2: row by row (stride 4 bytes): one miss per 64-byte block (16 elements): 16,384/16 = **1024 misses**.\nStep 3: column by column (stride 128 × 4 = 512 bytes): each access touches a different block, and the 128 blocks of a column map onto only 32 of the 256 lines (index = (8 × row) mod 256), so they evict each other before reuse. **Every access misses: 16,384 misses**, 16 times more.\nRemedies: loop interchange, tiling (blocking). (For a 16 × 16 matrix that fits in the cache, both orders give only 16 misses.)\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) AMAT = hit time + miss rate × miss penalty = 1 + 0.05 × 100 = 1 + 5 = **6 ns**.\n(b) Step 1: the L1 miss penalty = L2 hit time + local L2 miss rate × memory time = 10 + 0.20 × 100 = 30 ns.\nStep 2: AMAT = 1 + 0.10 × 30 = **4 ns**.\n(c) Step 1: memory stall cycles per instruction = 0.30 × 0.04 × 50 = 0.6.\nStep 2: **CPI = 1 + 0.6 = 1.6** (ignoring instruction-cache misses): the memory system adds 60 percent to the execution time.\n\n**Q5.** **Write-through:** every write updates both the cache and memory: simple and memory is always consistent, but the write traffic is high and writes are slow unless a **write buffer** holds the pending writes so the CPU does not wait. **Write-back:** a write hit updates only the cache and sets a **dirty bit**; the block is written to memory only when it is evicted: far less traffic and faster writes, but memory may hold stale data (a problem for DMA and multiprocessors, solved by coherence protocols) and the control is more complex. On a write miss, **write-allocate** loads the block into the cache first (typical with write-back); **no-write-allocate** writes directly to memory (typical with write-through).\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Step 1: 4 KB = 2^12, so the offset is **12 bits** and the virtual page number is 32 - 12 = **20 bits**.\nStep 2: a flat page table has 2^20 entries × 4 bytes = **4 MB per process**.\n(b) Step 1: a table with 10 index bits has 2^10 entries × 4 B = 4 KB (one page).\nStep 2: 8 MB/4 KB = 2048 pages; each second-level table maps 1024 pages (4 MB), so 2 second-level tables are needed.\nStep 3: total = 4 KB (top level) + 2 × 4 KB = **12 KB** instead of 4 MB.\n(c) **TLB hit:** the virtual page number is found in the TLB, giving the physical frame; the physical address is formed and the cache is accessed (a few cycles). **TLB miss:** the hardware or the OS walks the page table in memory (one access per level), loads the translation into the TLB and retries. **Page fault:** the page-table entry is invalid (the page is not in memory): the OS traps, finds a free frame (evicting a page if needed, writing it back if dirty), reads the page from disk (milliseconds), updates the page table and restarts the instruction.\n(d) Step 1: EAT = (1 - p) × 200 ns + p × 8 ms with 8 ms = 8,000,000 ns.\nStep 2: = 0.999999 × 200 + 10^-6 × 8,000,000 = 199.9998 + 8 = **208 ns** (a 4 percent slowdown from one fault per million accesses: page faults must be extremely rare).\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) Step 1: cycles spent polling per second = 1000 × 100 = 100,000.\nStep 2: CPU cycles per second = 100 × 10^6, so the share = 100,000/10^8 = 0.001 = **0.1 percent** (1 ms per second).\n(b) Step 1: interrupts: 100 events/s × 2000 cycles = 200,000 cycles/s = 0.2 percent of the CPU, and the CPU does other work in between instead of spinning.\nStep 2: for a rarely active device interrupts avoid the cost of frequent polling, but for **high data rates** (disks, network cards) one interrupt per word overwhelms the CPU; **DMA** transfers whole blocks directly between the device and memory and interrupts the CPU only once per block.\n\n**Q8.**\n(a) Amdahl: speedup = 1/(s + (1 - s)/N) with s = 0.05.\nStep 1: N = 8: 1/(0.05 + 0.95/8) = 1/(0.05 + 0.11875) = 1/0.16875 = **5.93**.\nStep 2: N = 1000: 1/(0.05 + 0.00095) = 1/0.05095 = **19.6**.\nStep 3: limit (N → ∞) = 1/0.05 = **20**, regardless of the number of cores.\n(b) Each core has its own private cache, so several cores may hold copies of the same memory block. A write by one core would leave stale copies in the others. A **coherence protocol** (for example MESI with snooping or a directory) invalidates or updates other copies so that all cores see a consistent value.\n(c) Examples: image and video processing (the same filter on every pixel), matrix multiplication and neural-network inference, vector additions, particle simulations, FFTs.","exam":"# Computer Organization: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and the RISC-V reference card allowed. Total: 100 marks. Assume RV32I and the classic five-stage pipeline (IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB) unless stated.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Which change reduces CPU time without changing the instruction count?\nA) a lower CPI  B) a larger program  C) more instructions per task  D) a longer clock period\n\n**2.** Dynamic power in CMOS is proportional to\nA) C V f  B) C V² f  C) V / f  D) C / V\n\n**3.** The hexadecimal word 0x000000FF in 32-bit two's complement is\nA) -1  B) 255  C) -255  D) 127\n\n**4.** The RISC-V instruction `jal ra, label` does what?\nA) branches if equal  B) jumps to label and saves the return address in ra  C) loads a word  D) returns from a procedure\n\n**5.** To return from a procedure in RISC-V one uses\nA) jal x0, ra  B) ret (jalr x0, 0(ra))  C) lw ra, 0(sp)  D) beq ra, x0\n\n**6.** The stack in RISC-V conventionally\nA) grows towards higher addresses  B) grows towards lower addresses  C) is in the register file  D) is read-only\n\n**7.** In IEEE 754 single precision, the pattern 0x3F800000 is\nA) 0  B) 1.0  C) 2.0  D) NaN\n\n**8.** In a single-cycle processor the clock period is determined by\nA) the average instruction  B) the shortest instruction  C) the longest instruction  D) the number of registers\n\n**9.** For the instruction `sw`, the control signal MemWrite is\nA) 0  B) 1  C) don't care  D) high impedance\n\n**10.** An ideal 5-stage pipeline (no hazards) completes n instructions in\nA) 5n cycles  B) n + 4 cycles  C) n cycles  D) 5 cycles\n\n**11.** A RAW hazard means\nA) read after write  B) write after write  C) write after read  D) read after read\n\n**12.** A branch predictor that uses a 2-bit saturating counter\nA) changes its prediction after one wrong outcome  B) needs two wrong outcomes in a row to change a strong prediction  C) never mispredicts  D) predicts always taken\n\n**13.** Associativity reduces mainly which kind of miss?\nA) compulsory  B) conflict  C) capacity  D) coherence\n\n**14.** For a fixed cache capacity, doubling the block size (direct-mapped) leads to\nA) more index bits  B) fewer index bits and more offset bits  C) more tag bits  D) no change\n\n**15.** A write-through cache with a write buffer\nA) never writes to memory  B) writes every store to memory (through the buffer)  C) uses dirty bits  D) cannot be used with DMA\n\n**16.** A page fault occurs when\nA) the TLB hits  B) the required page is not in physical memory  C) the cache misses  D) the CPU is idle\n\n**17.** With 32-bit virtual addresses and 4 KB pages the number of virtual pages is\nA) 2^12  B) 2^20  C) 2^32  D) 2^10\n\n**18.** Polling I/O differs from interrupt-driven I/O in that polling\nA) is initiated by the device  B) is initiated by the CPU repeatedly checking a status register  C) uses DMA  D) needs no CPU\n\n**19.** If a program is 10 percent serial, the maximum speedup on any number of cores is\nA) 5  B) 10  C) 100  D) unlimited\n\n**20.** The MESI protocol is used to\nA) predict branches  B) keep the caches of several cores coherent  C) translate addresses  D) schedule instructions\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** The 32-bit word 0xFFFFFFF6 is a two's complement integer. Give its decimal value and explain how you found it.\n\n**B2. (5)** Give the IEEE 754 single-precision hex representation of 0.15625 and show the steps.\n\n**B3. (5)** Encode `xori x7, x8, 15` (given in hex as 0x00F44393) and decode it back field by field.\n\n**B4. (5)** A pipeline has a base CPI of 1. 20 percent of the instructions are branches and 25 percent of them are mispredicted, with a 3-cycle penalty. Loads are 25 percent of instructions and 40 percent of loads cause a one-cycle load-use stall. Find the CPI.\n\n**B5. (5)** A 64 KB 2-way set-associative cache with 64-byte blocks and 32-bit addresses: find the number of sets and the widths of the tag, index and offset.\n\n**B6. (5)** With 4 KB pages and 32-bit virtual addresses, the physical address space is 1 GB. Find the number of physical frames, the physical page number width, and the size of a flat page table with 4-byte entries.\n\n**B7. (5)** A cache has a hit time of 2 cycles, a miss rate of 3 percent and a miss penalty of 100 cycles. Find the AMAT. If a second-level cache is added with a 12-cycle hit time and a local miss rate of 40 percent (the memory access then costs 100 cycles), find the new AMAT.\n\n**B8. (5)** Explain the difference between latency and throughput in a pipeline, with the figures for a 5-stage pipeline with 200 ps stages.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. Assembly programming.** Write RISC-V code that multiplies x10 by x11 using repeated addition, leaving the result in x12 (assume x11 ≥ 0).\n(a) Write the code [5]. (b) For x10 = 6 and x11 = 7 give the result and the number of instructions executed (count the setup instructions and the final nop) [3]. (c) Explain how a faster multiplication method works and its cost [2].\n\n**C2. Pipeline analysis.** Consider `lw x5, 0(x1); add x6, x5, x2; sub x7, x6, x3; lw x8, 4(x1); and x9, x8, x5`.\n(a) List the data dependences and mark the load-use hazards [3]. (b) Draw (as a table) the pipeline timing with full forwarding and give the total cycles [4]. (c) Reorder the instructions to remove the stalls and give the new cycle count [3].\n\n**C3. Cache simulation.** A cache has 4 blocks of 32 bytes. Byte addresses 0, 32, 64, 0, 96, 32, 128, 0, 64, 160 are accessed.\n(a) Give the block addresses [1]. (b) Trace a direct-mapped cache (hit/miss sequence and hit count) [3]. (c) Trace a 2-way set-associative cache with LRU [3]. (d) Compute the hit rates and compare with the 4-way (fully associative) case [3].\n\n**C4. Floating point.**\n(a) Convert 0.5 and -0.4375 to IEEE 754 single precision (hex) [4]. (b) Add them step by step (align exponents, add, normalise) and give the result in hex [4]. (c) Explain why floating-point addition is not associative, giving an example [2].\n\n**C5. Virtual memory.** A system has 4 KB pages and 32-bit virtual addresses. The page table maps virtual page 0x00403 to frame 0x07B.\n(a) Split the virtual address 0x00403ABC into the page number and offset [2]. (b) Find the physical address [2]. (c) Describe what happens in the TLB and the page table if the TLB misses, and what if the page is not in memory [4]. (d) Compute the effective access time if a TLB hit takes 1 ns, a TLB miss adds 100 ns (the page table walk), the TLB hit rate is 99 percent and the memory access takes 100 ns [2].\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **A** · 2 **B** · 3 **B** · 4 **B** · 5 **B** · 6 **B** · 7 **B** · 8 **C** · 9 **B** · 10 **B** · 11 **A** · 12 **B** · 13 **B** · 14 **B** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **B** · 18 **B** · 19 **B** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons: 3. FF = 255 with zero upper bytes. 7. Exponent 127, fraction 0. 10. The first instruction takes 5 cycles and each later one adds one. 19. 1/0.1 = 10.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** 0xFFFFFFF6 has sign bit 1, so the number is negative.\nStep 1: invert all bits: 0xFFFFFFF6 → 0x00000009.\nStep 2: add 1: 0x00000009 + 1 = 0x0000000A = 10.\nStep 3: the value is **-10**.\nCheck: 0xFFFFFFF6 = 4,294,967,286 and 4,294,967,286 - 2^32 = **-10**.\n\n**B2.** Step 1: 0.15625 = 5/32 = 5 × 2⁻⁵ = 101₂ × 2⁻⁵ = 0.00101₂.\nStep 2: normalise: 0.00101₂ = 1.01₂ × 2⁻³.\nStep 3: sign 0; exponent = -3 + 127 = 124 = 01111100₂; fraction 01 followed by 21 zeros.\nStep 4: bits 0 01111100 01000000000000000000000 = 0011 1110 0010 0000 ... = **0x3E200000**.\n\n**B3.** Step 1: write 0x00F44393 in binary: 0000 0000 1111 0100 0100 0011 1001 0011.\nStep 2: split into I-type fields: imm[31:20] = 000000001111 = 15; rs1[19:15] = 01000 = 8; funct3[14:12] = 100 (xor); rd[11:7] = 00111 = 7; opcode[6:0] = 0010011 (OP-IMM).\nStep 3: so it is **xori x7, x8, 15**.\nCheck (encoding): 15 << 20 = 0x00F00000; rs1 8 << 15 = 0x00040000; funct3 4 << 12 = 0x4000; rd 7 << 7 = 0x380; opcode 0x13. Sum = 0x00F00000 + 0x00040000 + 0x4000 + 0x380 + 0x13 = 0x00F44393 ✓.\n\n**B4.** Step 1: branch penalty per instruction = 0.20 × 0.25 × 3 = 0.15.\nStep 2: load-use stalls per instruction = 0.25 × 0.40 × 1 = 0.10.\nStep 3: **CPI = 1 + 0.15 + 0.10 = 1.25**.\n\n**B5.** Step 1: lines = 64 KB/64 B = 65,536/64 = 1024.\nStep 2: sets = 1024/2 ways = **512**.\nStep 3: offset = log2 64 = **6 bits**; index = log2 512 = **9 bits**; tag = 32 - 6 - 9 = **17 bits**.\n\n**B6.** Step 1: frames = physical memory/page size = 1 GB/4 KB = 2^30/2^12 = **2^18 = 262,144 frames**.\nStep 2: physical page number width = **18 bits**.\nStep 3: virtual page number = 32 - 12 = 20 bits, so the flat page table has 2^20 entries.\nStep 4: size = 2^20 × 4 bytes = **4 MB**.\n\n**B7.** Step 1: AMAT (L1 only) = 2 + 0.03 × 100 = 2 + 3 = **5 cycles**.\nStep 2: with L2, the miss penalty seen by L1 = 12 + 0.40 × 100 = 12 + 40 = 52 cycles.\nStep 3: AMAT = 2 + 0.03 × 52 = 2 + 1.56 = **3.56 cycles**.\n\n**B8.** Step 1: **Latency** is the time for one instruction to complete from start to finish: in a 5-stage pipeline with 200 ps stages it is 5 × 200 = **1000 ps** (no better than a single-cycle machine's 800 ps; slightly worse because of pipeline register overhead and the stage balancing).\nStep 2: **Throughput** is the number of instructions completed per unit time: one per cycle, i.e. one every **200 ps**, which is 1/(200 × 10⁻¹²) = 5 × 10⁹ instructions/s, four times better than the single-cycle processor's one per 800 ps (1.25 × 10⁹ instructions/s).\nStep 3: Pipelining improves throughput, not latency.\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.**\n(a)\n```\n      li   x12, 0          # result = 0\n      mv   x13, x11        # counter = multiplier\nloop: beq  x13, x0, done   # while (counter != 0)\n      add  x12, x12, x10   # result += multiplicand\n      addi x13, x13, -1    # counter--\n      j    loop\ndone: nop\n```\n(b) With x10 = 6 and x11 = 7 (already in the registers):\nStep 1: each iteration adds 6 once; after 7 iterations x12 = 7 × 6 = **42**.\nStep 2: instruction count: 2 (setup: li, mv) + 7 iterations × 4 (beq, add, addi, j) = 28, + 1 (the final beq that exits) + 1 (the final nop) = 2 + 28 + 1 + 1 = **32 instructions**.\n(c) **Shift-and-add** (as in hardware multipliers) examines each bit of the multiplier: if the bit is 1, add the shifted multiplicand. A 32-bit multiplier needs at most 32 additions and shifts instead of up to 2^32 repeated additions. Hardware multipliers use arrays of adders or Wallace trees to do it in a few cycles; the M extension's `mul` instruction does this in one instruction.\n\n**C2.** Code: lw x5,0(x1); add x6,x5,x2; sub x7,x6,x3; lw x8,4(x1); and x9,x8,x5.\n(a) add reads x5 from the first lw (RAW, **load-use**); sub reads x6 from add (RAW, forwardable); and reads x8 from the second lw (RAW, **load-use**) and x5 from the first lw (a distance of four instructions: no hazard).\n(b) Step 1: with full forwarding, the add must wait one cycle for the lw (stall 1).\nStep 2: the sub needs add's result (forwarded, no stall); the second lw is independent.\nStep 3: the and needs x8 from the lw directly before it (stall 1).\nTiming table (cycles 1 to 11):\n- lw x5: IF 1, ID 2, EX 3, MEM 4, WB 5\n- add x6: IF 2, ID 3, ID 4 (stall: the loaded x5 is not ready), EX 5, MEM 6, WB 7\n- sub x7: IF 3 (held until 4), ID 5, EX 6, MEM 7, WB 8 (x6 is forwarded from add)\n- lw x8: IF 5, ID 6, EX 7, MEM 8, WB 9\n- and x9: IF 6, ID 7, ID 8 (stall: x8 is loaded by the previous instruction), EX 9, MEM 10, WB 11\nTotal **11 cycles** (ideal 5 + 4 = 9, plus 2 stalls).\n(c) Move the independent `lw x8, 4(x1)` up: `lw x5, 0(x1); lw x8, 4(x1); add x6, x5, x2; sub x7, x6, x3; and x9, x8, x5`. Now each load's result is used at least two instructions later: **no stalls, 5 + 4 = 9 cycles**.\n\n**C3.**\n(a) Block addresses (byte address ÷ 32): 0, 32, 64, 0, 96, 32, 128, 0, 64, 160 → **0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 1, 4, 0, 2, 5**.\n(b) Direct-mapped (4 lines, index = block mod 4): indices 0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 1, 0, 0, 2, 1.\nStep 1: block 0 miss; block 1 miss; block 2 miss.\nStep 2: block 0 **hit** (index 0 still holds 0); block 3 miss; block 1 **hit**.\nStep 3: block 4 (index 0) miss, evicting 0; block 0 (index 0) miss, evicting 4.\nStep 4: block 2 **hit**; block 5 (index 1) miss.\nSequence **M M M H M H M M H M**: **3 hits**.\n(c) 2-way (2 sets, index = block mod 2, LRU): the even blocks 0, 2, 4 share set 0 and the odd blocks 1, 3, 5 share set 1.\nStep 1: 0 M, 1 M, 2 M, 0 **H** (set 0 now ordered: 2 is least recently used).\nStep 2: 3 M, 1 **H**.\nStep 3: 4 M (evicts the least recently used block 2 from set 0), 0 **H** (still present).\nStep 4: 2 M (evicts 4), 5 M.\nThe sequence is **M M M H M H M H M M**: **3 hits**, the same count as the direct-mapped cache, but different hits (the eighth access now hits, the ninth now misses).\n(d) Step 1: hit rate = hits/accesses = 3/10 = **30 percent** for both organisations.\nStep 2: the fully associative (4-way) cache also gives **M M M H M H M H M M**, 3 hits = 30 percent (when block 4 arrives, the least recently used block 2 is evicted).\nStep 3: for this short sequence the extra associativity helps only slightly because most misses are **compulsory** (blocks 0 to 5 are each touched for the first time: 6 compulsory misses); the remaining misses are capacity or conflict misses.\n\n**C4.**\n(a) Step 1: 0.5 = 1.0 × 2⁻¹: sign 0, exponent -1 + 127 = 126 = 01111110₂, fraction 0: bits 0 01111110 000... = **0x3F000000**.\nStep 2: 0.4375 = 7/16 = 0.0111₂ = 1.11₂ × 2⁻² (1.75 × 0.25).\nStep 3: sign 1, exponent -2 + 127 = 125 = 01111101₂, fraction 11 followed by zeros: bits 1 01111101 11000... = **0xBEE00000**.\n(b) Step 1: align the exponents: 0.5 = 1.000 × 2⁻¹; -0.4375 = -1.11 × 2⁻² = -0.111 × 2⁻¹ (shift the significand right by 1).\nStep 2: add the significands (subtraction because of the sign): 1.000 - 0.111 = 0.001 (× 2⁻¹).\nStep 3: normalise: 0.001 × 2⁻¹ = 1.0 × 2⁻⁴ (shift left 3 places, decrease the exponent by 3).\nStep 4: exponent = -4 + 127 = 123 = 01111011₂, fraction 0: **0x3D800000** = 0.0625.\nCheck: 0.5 - 0.4375 = 0.0625 ✓.\n(c) Rounding makes the result depend on the order of the operations. In single precision the spacing between numbers near 10^8 is 8, so adding 1 to 10^8 changes nothing.\nStep 1: (10^8 + (-10^8)) + 1 = 0 + 1 = **1**.\nStep 2: 10^8 + ((-10^8) + 1) = 10^8 + (-10^8) = **0**.\nThe two groupings give different results, so floating-point addition is **not associative**; programs must not rely on exact equality or on a particular summation order (for accuracy, sum small numbers first or use compensated summation).\n\n**C5.**\n(a) Step 1: 4 KB pages: offset = low 12 bits = 3 hex digits: **0xABC**.\nStep 2: virtual page number = upper 20 bits = **0x00403**.\n(b) Step 1: the page table gives frame 0x07B for page 0x00403.\nStep 2: physical address = (0x07B << 12) | 0xABC = 0x7B000 + 0xABC = **0x0007BABC**.\n(c) **TLB miss:** the hardware (or the OS) walks the page table in memory using the virtual page number, finds that the entry is valid and has frame 0x07B, installs the translation in the TLB and retries; the access then proceeds with the physical address. **Page fault** (the entry is invalid because the page is not in memory): the CPU traps to the OS, which finds a free frame (evicting another page, writing it back if it is dirty), reads the page from disk (milliseconds), updates the page table and TLB, and restarts the faulting instruction.\n(d) Step 1: average translation cost = 1 ns + 0.01 × 100 ns = 1 + 1 = 2.0 ns.\nStep 2: add the data access of 100 ns: EAT = 2.0 + 100 = **102 ns**.\nWithout a TLB every access would cost 200 ns (page table + data), so the TLB nearly halves the access time.\n","notes":"# Computer Organization: Complete Lecture Notes\n\nComputer organization explains how a processor actually works: how programs become bits, how bits flow through a datapath, how pipelines and caches make machines fast, and how memory and I/O fit around the CPU. The reference architecture is **RISC-V (RV32I)**, a clean modern instruction set. Terms: ISA (instruction set architecture, the contract between software and hardware), CPI (cycles per instruction), IC (instruction count), T (clock period), f (clock frequency).\n\n## Chapter 1. Performance and Cost\n\n### 1.1 Measuring performance\n- **Execution time** is the real metric. **CPU time = IC × CPI × T = IC × CPI/f.** Improving performance means reducing IC (better compiler, better ISA), CPI (better microarchitecture) or T (faster technology).\n- **Example.** An instruction mix: ALU 50 percent (CPI 1), loads 20 percent (CPI 2), stores 10 percent (CPI 2), branches 20 percent (CPI 3): CPI = 0.5 + 0.4 + 0.2 + 0.6 = 1.7. At 2 GHz, 10^9 instructions take 10^9 × 1.7/(2 × 10^9) = 0.85 s, and the rate is 2000/1.7 = 1176 MIPS.\n- **Speedup** = time_before/time_after. MIPS and clock rate alone are misleading; use benchmark suites (SPEC) and report the geometric mean.\n- **Amdahl's law:** speeding up a fraction F of the execution by a factor S gives Speedup = 1/((1 - F) + F/S). A 10× faster component used 40 percent of the time gives 1/(0.6 + 0.04) = 1.56×. The unimproved part limits the gain: make the common case fast.\n- **Power and energy:** dynamic power P = α C V² f (activity factor α, switched capacitance C, voltage V, frequency f). Lowering V helps quadratically, which is why the \"power wall\" ended frequency scaling and led to multicore processors. Energy = power × time.\n\n### 1.2 Number representation recap\nBytes, words, hexadecimal (each hex digit is 4 bits). **Endianness:** the word 0x12345678 stored at address A is 78 56 34 12 in memory order in **little-endian** (RISC-V, x86) and 12 34 56 78 in big-endian (network order). Unsigned and two's complement integers (see Digital Logic); sign extension; overflow detection.\n\n## Chapter 2. The Instruction Set (RISC-V RV32I)\n\n### 2.1 Design principles\nSimplicity favours regularity; smaller is faster (few registers, simple instructions); make the common case fast; good design demands good compromises. RISC-V has **32 registers** x0 to x31 (32 bits each), where **x0 is always 0**. ABI names: ra (x1, return address), sp (x2, stack pointer), a0 to a7 (x10 to x17, arguments and return values), t0 to t6 (temporaries), s0 to s11 (saved registers). All instructions are 32 bits, and arithmetic is done on registers (**load-store architecture**).\n\n### 2.2 Instruction formats and encoding\n- **R-type** (register-register): funct7[31:25] rs2[24:20] rs1[19:15] funct3[14:12] rd[11:7] opcode[6:0]. add x5, x6, x7 = 0x007302B3; sub x8, x9, x10 = 0x40A48433.\n- **I-type** (immediate and loads): imm[11:0] rs1 funct3 rd opcode. addi x1, x0, 5 = 0x00500093; addi x5, x6, -1 = 0xFFF30293; lw x10, 8(x2) = 0x00812503.\n- **S-type** (stores): imm[11:5] rs2 rs1 funct3 imm[4:0] opcode. sw x10, 12(x2) = 0x00A12623.\n- **B-type** (branches): a 13-bit signed PC-relative offset (multiples of 2) split across the word; **J-type** (jal) a 21-bit offset; **U-type** (lui, auipc) a 20-bit upper immediate.\n- **Loading a 32-bit constant:** lui x5, 0x12345 followed by addi x5, x5, 0x678 gives 0x12345678 (if the low 12 bits have the top bit set, add 1 to the upper part to compensate for the sign extension).\n\n### 2.3 The common instructions\n- **Arithmetic and logic:** add, sub, and, or, xor, sll, srl, sra, slt (set if less than), and immediate versions addi, andi, ori, xori, slli, srli, srai, slti; the M extension adds mul, div, rem.\n- **Memory:** lw (load word), sw, lh/lhu, sh, lb/lbu, sb. Address = base register + signed 12-bit offset. Arrays: element i of a word array at base is at base + 4i (slli by 2).\n- **Control:** beq, bne, blt, bge (signed), bltu, bgeu; jal rd, label (jump and link, with rd = ra for calls); jalr rd, offset(rs1) (indirect jump, used for returns: `ret` = jalr x0, 0(ra)). Pseudo-instructions: li, mv, j, nop, la, call, ret.\n\n### 2.4 Translating programs\n```\n# Sum of the 4-word array at address 256: x5 = sum\n        li   x5, 0          # sum\n        li   x6, 0          # i\n        li   x7, 4          # n\n        li   x8, 256        # base address\nloop:   bge  x6, x7, done   # while (i < n)\n        slli x9, x6, 2      # offset = 4 * i\n        add  x9, x9, x8     # address of a[i]\n        lw   x10, 0(x9)\n        add  x5, x5, x10    # sum += a[i]\n        addi x6, x6, 1      # i++\n        j    loop\ndone:\n```\nWith the array {3, 5, 7, 9} the result is 24 and the loop executes 34 instructions in total (4 setup, 7 per iteration, a final test and the exit). Other programs: maximum of an array (9 for {4, 9, 2, 7}), iterative Fibonacci (F(10) = 55 in 66 instructions), the subtractive GCD (gcd(48, 18) = 6).\n- **Bitwise operations** with x5 = 12 (1100) and x6 = 5 (0101): add 17, sub 7, and 4, or 13, xor 9, shift left by 3 gives 96, shift right by 2 gives 3, slt (5 < 12) gives 1.\n\n### 2.5 Procedures and the stack\nProcedure call: put arguments in a0 to a7; `jal ra, func` saves the return address; the callee computes and puts the result in a0; `ret` returns. A procedure that calls another must save ra (and the s-registers it uses) on the **stack** (a region of memory growing downwards): `addi sp, sp, -16; sw ra, 12(sp) ... lw ra, 12(sp); addi sp, sp, 16`. Calling conventions define which registers the caller (t, a registers) and the callee (s registers, sp, ra) must preserve. Recursion works because each activation has its own **stack frame** (saved registers, local variables). Example: `li a0, 5; jal ra, dbl; ...; dbl: slli a0, a0, 1; ret` returns 10 in a0.\n**Memory layout:** text (code), static data, heap (grows up), stack (grows down). **Addressing modes in RISC-V:** register, immediate, base + displacement, PC-relative.\n\n### 2.6 Other ISAs and the toolchain\nx86-64 (CISC, variable length, micro-ops), ARM (RISC), MIPS. The **compiler → assembler → linker → loader** chain; assembly directives; object files and relocation; the **simulator** (Venus, RARS, Spike) for experiments.\n\n## Chapter 3. Computer Arithmetic\n\n- **Addition and subtraction:** a ripple or carry-lookahead adder; subtraction adds the two's complement. **Overflow** when the operands have the same sign and the result has the opposite sign; RISC-V does not trap, software checks.\n- **Multiplication:** shift-and-add: for each multiplier bit, add the shifted multiplicand; a 32 × 32 product needs 64 bits. Hardware uses an array or a Wallace tree; **Booth recoding** reduces additions for runs of ones. **Division:** shift-and-subtract (restoring/non-restoring); the M extension provides mul, mulh, div, rem.\n- **Floating point (IEEE 754):** value = (-1)^s × 1.f × 2^(e - bias). Single precision: 1 sign bit, 8 exponent bits (bias 127), 23 fraction bits; double: 1, 11 (bias 1023), 52. Special values: exponent all ones (infinity, NaN), exponent zero (zero and subnormals). Examples: 1.0 = 0x3F800000; 13.25 = 0x41540000 (1.10101 × 2³); -0.1 = 0xBDCCCCCD (not exactly representable); 1.5 = 0x3FC00000; 2.25 = 0x40100000; and 1.5 + 2.25 = 3.75 = 0x40700000.\n- **Floating-point addition:** align exponents (shift the smaller), add significands, normalise, round (to nearest even). FP is **not associative**: (x + y) + z may differ from x + (y + z); never test equality with ==.\n\n## Chapter 4. The Processor: Datapath and Control\n\n### 4.1 Building blocks\nRegister file (two read ports, one write port), ALU, instruction and data memories, program counter (PC) and its adder, sign extender, multiplexers, control unit. Clocked state elements (PC, registers) are updated on the clock edge; combinational logic computes in between.\n\n### 4.2 Single-cycle datapath\nOne instruction per clock cycle. The cycle time is set by the **slowest instruction (the lw)**. With component delays of instruction fetch 200 ps, register read 100 ps, ALU 200 ps, data memory 200 ps and register write 100 ps: lw = 800 ps, sw = 700 ps, R-type = 600 ps, beq = 500 ps. The clock must be 800 ps although most instructions are faster: inefficient.\n- **Control signals:** RegWrite, ALUSrc, ALUOp, MemRead, MemWrite, MemtoReg, Branch, PCSrc. The main control decodes the opcode; the ALU control uses funct3/funct7. For lw: ALUSrc = 1 (immediate), ALU adds, MemRead = 1, MemtoReg = 1, RegWrite = 1. For beq: ALU subtracts, Branch = 1.\n- **Multicycle** designs reuse hardware and give each instruction as many short cycles as it needs. **Microprogrammed control** (ROM of micro-instructions) and **hardwired control** (logic) are the two ways to implement the control unit.\n\n## Chapter 5. Pipelining\n\n### 5.1 The idea\nOverlap the execution of instructions like an assembly line. The classic five stages: **IF** (fetch), **ID** (decode and register read), **EX** (execute, address calculation), **MEM** (data memory access), **WB** (write back). Pipeline registers hold intermediate values between stages. With balanced stages (200 ps) the clock falls from 800 ps to 200 ps; the ideal speedup approaches the number of stages (5) and the CPI approaches 1: n instructions take (n + 4) cycles. For 1000 instructions: single-cycle 800 ns versus pipelined (1000 + 4) × 200 ps = 200.8 ns: speedup 3.98 (the latency of one instruction is not improved; the **throughput** is).\n\n### 5.2 Hazards\n- **Structural hazards:** two instructions need the same hardware in one cycle (a single memory for instructions and data). Solved by separate instruction and data caches and a register file with separate read and write ports.\n- **Data hazards:** an instruction needs a result that an earlier instruction has not yet written. Types: RAW (read after write, the real dependence), WAR and WAW (only in out-of-order machines).\n - **Forwarding (bypassing)** sends results from the EX/MEM and MEM/WB pipeline registers back to the ALU inputs, removing most stalls.\n - **Load-use hazard:** a load's data is ready only at the end of MEM, so an instruction that uses it in the very next cycle must **stall one cycle** even with forwarding.\n - Without forwarding (but with a register file written in the first half and read in the second half of a cycle), a dependent instruction right behind its producer stalls 2 cycles. For `lw x1, 0(x2); add x3, x1, x4; sub x5, x3, x6; sw x5, 0(x7)`: ideal 8 cycles (5 + 3); with forwarding 9 (one load-use stall); without forwarding 14 (three dependences, 2 stalls each).\n - **Compiler scheduling** can fill load delay slots by reordering independent instructions.\n- **Control hazards:** a branch is resolved after the fetch of following instructions. Solutions: stall, **branch prediction** (static: predict not taken or backward-taken; dynamic: a branch history table with 1-bit or 2-bit saturating counters and a branch target buffer), and flushing the wrongly fetched instructions (a misprediction penalty of 1 to 3 cycles in a 5-stage pipe, 15 or more in deep modern pipelines). Effective CPI = 1 + (branch frequency × misprediction rate × penalty): with 20 percent branches, 10 percent mispredicted and a 2-cycle penalty, CPI = 1 + 0.2 × 0.1 × 2 = 1.04.\n- **Exceptions and interrupts** need precise state: the pipeline flushes later instructions and records the PC in a register (mepc).\n\n### 5.3 Beyond the simple pipeline\n**Deeper pipelines** raise the clock but increase hazard penalties. **Superscalar** processors issue several instructions per cycle (ILP); **out-of-order execution** with register renaming and a reorder buffer (Tomasulo's algorithm) hides latencies; **speculative execution** guesses branch outcomes (and the security issues it caused: Spectre and Meltdown). **VLIW** puts the scheduling burden on the compiler.\n\n## Chapter 6. The Memory Hierarchy\n\n### 6.1 Locality and the hierarchy\n**Temporal locality:** recently used items are used again soon. **Spatial locality:** items near recently used ones are used soon. The hierarchy: registers (under 1 ns) → L1 cache (SRAM, about 1 ns, tens of KB) → L2/L3 (several MB, 5 to 30 ns) → main memory (DRAM, about 50 to 100 ns, GB) → SSD/disk (microseconds to milliseconds, TB). SRAM is fast and expensive (6 transistors per bit); DRAM needs refreshing (1 transistor and a capacitor).\n\n### 6.2 Caches\nA cache holds **blocks (lines)** of main memory. An address splits into **tag | index | block offset**. For a 32-bit address:\n- **Direct-mapped:** each block maps to exactly one line (index = block address mod number of lines). A 16 KB cache with 64-byte blocks has 256 lines: offset = 6 bits, index = 8 bits, tag = 18 bits; each line stores a valid bit, a tag and 512 data bits (1 + 18 + 512 = 531 bits per line), so the total is 135,936 bits (about 17 KB of SRAM for 16 KB of data).\n- **Set-associative (n-way):** a block can go in any of the n lines of one set (index selects the set). A 32 KB 4-way cache with 64-byte blocks has 128 sets: offset 6, index 7, tag 19. **Fully associative:** any block in any line (tag = block address; used for TLBs).\n- **Replacement:** LRU, FIFO, random. **Write policies:** write-through (write to memory too, often with a write buffer) versus write-back (a dirty bit; write on eviction); on a write miss, write-allocate versus no-write-allocate.\n- **Miss types (the three Cs):** compulsory (first reference), capacity (cache too small), conflict (too many blocks mapping to one set). More associativity reduces conflicts; larger blocks reduce compulsory misses via spatial locality but increase the miss penalty.\n- **Worked trace.** A direct-mapped cache with 8 blocks of 16 bytes and byte addresses 0, 4, 16, 132, 232, 160, 1024, 30, 140, 3100, 180, 2180 gives M H M M M M M H M M M M (2 hits of 12). The same sequence with 2-way associativity gives 3 hits. A sequential read of 64 bytes word by word with 16-byte blocks gives one miss per block: M H H H repeated, a miss rate of 25 percent. Two arrays 1024 bytes apart accessed alternately in a 1 KB direct-mapped cache thrash (every access misses); a 2-way cache avoids this.\n\n### 6.3 Cache performance\n**AMAT = hit time + miss rate × miss penalty.** With a 1 ns hit time, a 5 percent miss rate and a 100 ns penalty: AMAT = 1 + 0.05 × 100 = 6 ns. **Two-level:** AMAT = L1 hit + L1 miss rate × (L2 hit + L2 miss rate × memory): 1 + 0.1(10 + 0.2 × 100) = 4 ns. **CPI with memory stalls** = base CPI + (memory accesses per instruction) × miss rate × miss penalty (in cycles). Software optimisation: loop interchange and blocking (tiling) for matrix code, data layout, prefetching. **Cache coherence** (MESI protocol) keeps the caches of multiple cores consistent.\n\n### 6.4 Virtual memory\nEach process has a **virtual address space** mapped to physical memory in **pages** (typically 4 KB). The OS maintains a **page table** (virtual page number → physical frame, with valid, dirty and protection bits). With 4 KB pages and 32-bit addresses: 12-bit offset and a 20-bit virtual page number, so a flat page table has 2^20 entries (4 MB at 4 bytes per entry); multi-level and inverted page tables reduce the size. A **TLB** (translation look-aside buffer) caches recent translations; a TLB miss walks the page table; a **page fault** (page not in memory) is handled by the OS, which loads the page from disk (millions of cycles). Benefits: isolation and protection, relocation, more memory than physical RAM (demand paging), sharing. Page replacement: LRU approximations (clock).\n\n## Chapter 7. Input/Output and Storage\n- **Buses** connect the CPU, memory and I/O; memory-mapped I/O assigns device registers to addresses. **Polling** (the CPU checks status) versus **interrupts** (the device signals the CPU, which runs an interrupt service routine) versus **DMA** (direct memory access: a controller moves blocks without the CPU).\n- **Storage:** HDD (seek time + rotational latency + transfer), SSD (flash, no moving parts, wear levelling); RAID levels trade capacity, reliability and speed. Examples of buses and interfaces: PCIe, USB, SATA/NVMe, I2C/SPI/UART (embedded).\n\n## Chapter 8. Parallel Processors\n**Flynn's taxonomy:** SISD, SIMD (vector units, GPUs), MIMD (multicore, clusters). Multicore processors use threads and shared memory; challenges: synchronisation, cache coherence, memory consistency, Amdahl's law (the serial fraction limits the speedup: with 5 percent serial code the maximum speedup is 20 regardless of core count). **Data parallelism** (SIMD, GPUs) suits regular numerical work. Energy efficiency motivates heterogeneous systems (big.LITTLE, accelerators).\n\n## Chapter 9. Common Mistakes Checklist\n1. Forgetting that x0 is hard-wired to zero and that branch offsets are relative to the branch instruction itself.\n2. Mixing up byte addresses and word indices (forgetting to multiply the index by 4).\n3. Ignoring the sign extension of the 12-bit immediate when building 32-bit constants.\n4. Counting pipeline cycles incorrectly: n instructions need n + 4 cycles in a 5-stage pipeline, not 5n; a load-use stall remains even with forwarding.\n5. Mislabelling the tag, index and offset fields (a larger block means more offset bits and fewer index bits for a fixed capacity).\n6. Using CPI, clock rate or MIPS alone to compare machines.\n7. Assuming floating-point arithmetic is exact or associative.\n8. Forgetting to save and restore ra and the s-registers in nested procedure calls.\n","plan":"# Computer Organization: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 11 hours per week (3 reading, 5 to 6 problems and programming, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Learn this course by **running code on a simulator** and by **drawing the hardware**. Install a RISC-V simulator now (Venus or RARS are easy), and a cache-simulation tool or write your own in Python.\n\n## Textbooks\n- David Patterson and John Hennessy, *Computer Organization and Design: RISC-V Edition* (Morgan Kaufmann). The standard text for this course.\n- Hennessy and Patterson, *Computer Architecture: A Quantitative Approach* (later, for depth).\n- Harris and Harris, *Digital Design and Computer Architecture: RISC-V Edition*.\n- Randal Bryant and David O'Hallaron, *Computer Systems: A Programmer's Perspective* (how software meets hardware).\n- Andrew Waterman and Krste Asanović, *The RISC-V Instruction Set Manual* (free; the specification).\n\n## Free courses and tools\n- UC Berkeley CS61C Great Ideas in Computer Architecture (lectures and labs online).\n- MIT OpenCourseWare 6.004 Computation Structures; ETH Zurich Digital Design and Computer Architecture lectures (Onur Mutlu, on YouTube).\n- Nand2Tetris (part 2) for building a CPU and an assembler.\n- Venus, RARS and Ripes (a graphical RISC-V pipeline simulator); Compiler Explorer (godbolt.org) to see compiled assembly; Cachegrind or a Python cache simulator.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Performance and measurement (Ch 1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1. Practise CPI, Amdahl and power calculations.\n\n**Week 2: Data representation: endianness, two's complement, IEEE 754 (Ch 1.2, 3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q2. Write a program that prints the bytes of an int and a float.\n\n**Week 3: RISC-V instructions and formats (Ch 2.1 to 2.3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q3. Encode and decode ten instructions by hand.\n\n**Week 4: Assembly programming: loops, arrays (Ch 2.4).** Do: Assignment 1 Q4, Q5. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week.\n\n**Week 5: Procedures, the stack, calling conventions (Ch 2.5).** Do: Assignment 1 Q6. Write a recursive factorial and Fibonacci in assembly and check the stack in the simulator.\n\n**Week 6: Arithmetic: multiplication, division, floating point (Ch 3).** Implement shift-and-add multiplication in assembly.\n\n**Week 7: Single-cycle datapath and control (Ch 4).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1, Q2. Draw the datapath for lw and beq from memory.\n\n**Week 8: Pipelining and hazards (Ch 5.1, 5.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q3, Q4. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 9: Branch prediction, CPI and advanced pipelines (Ch 5.2, 5.3).** Do: Assignment 2 Q5 to Q7.\n\n**Week 10: Caches (Ch 6.1, 6.2).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1, Q2. Implement a cache simulator in Python.\n\n**Week 11: Cache performance and locality (Ch 6.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q3, Q4, Q5. Time row-major versus column-major matrix traversal on your laptop.\n\n**Week 12: Virtual memory (Ch 6.4).** Do: Assignment 3 Q6.\n\n**Week 13: I/O, DMA, parallel processors and coherence (Ch 7, 8).** Do: Assignment 3 Q7, Q8.\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, with the RISC-V reference card).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can compute CPU time, CPI, speedup (Amdahl) and relative power.\n- [ ] I can convert between hexadecimal, two's complement and IEEE 754 formats.\n- [ ] I can encode and decode RISC-V instructions in all the basic formats.\n- [ ] I can write and trace assembly with loops, arrays, procedures and the stack.\n- [ ] I can draw the single-cycle datapath and give the control signals for common instructions.\n- [ ] I can analyse a pipeline: stages, hazards, forwarding, stalls, branch prediction, CPI.\n- [ ] I can decompose addresses for direct-mapped and set-associative caches and trace hits and misses.\n- [ ] I can compute AMAT and CPI with memory stalls, and explain write policies.\n- [ ] I can explain virtual memory, page tables and the TLB, and compute their sizes.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with the reference card.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Always execute programs in a simulator and compare with your hand trace.\n2. Draw timing diagrams for pipelines: stage by stage and cycle by cycle.\n3. For cache questions, write the tag/index/offset split first, then step through each access.\n4. Keep a one-page summary of formats and conventions (instruction formats, register names, ABI rules).\n5. Connect the topics: compilers produce the instruction mix, the pipeline sets the CPI, the caches decide the stalls.\n","test":"# Computer Organization: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Assume RISC-V RV32I and a five-stage pipeline (IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB).\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** CPU time equals\nA) IC × CPI × clock period  B) IC / CPI  C) CPI / f  D) IC + CPI\n\n**2.** Amdahl's law says that the overall speedup from improving part of a program is limited by\nA) the clock rate  B) the fraction of time not improved  C) the cache size  D) the number of registers\n\n**3.** In RISC-V, register x0\nA) holds the stack pointer  B) always reads as zero  C) holds the return address  D) is a temporary\n\n**4.** RISC-V RV32I has how many general-purpose registers?\nA) 8  B) 16  C) 32  D) 64\n\n**5.** `lw x5, 8(x2)` loads into x5\nA) the value x2 + 8  B) the word at memory address x2 + 8  C) the value 8  D) the address x2\n\n**6.** In 8-bit two's complement, -5 is\nA) 10000101  B) 11111010  C) 11111011  D) 00000101\n\n**7.** Which format does `add x5, x6, x7` use?\nA) I-type  B) R-type  C) S-type  D) B-type\n\n**8.** The five stages of the classic pipeline in order are\nA) IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB  B) ID, IF, EX, WB, MEM  C) IF, EX, ID, MEM, WB  D) IF, ID, MEM, EX, WB\n\n**9.** Forwarding (bypassing) is used to\nA) predict branches  B) supply a result to a dependent instruction before it is written to the register file  C) enlarge the cache  D) speed up the clock\n\n**10.** With forwarding, a load followed immediately by an instruction using the loaded value causes\nA) no stall  B) one stall cycle  C) two stall cycles  D) a pipeline flush\n\n**11.** A control hazard is caused by\nA) two instructions needing the same hardware  B) a dependence on a register  C) a branch whose outcome is not yet known  D) a cache miss\n\n**12.** Accessing an array sequentially exploits\nA) temporal locality only  B) spatial locality  C) no locality  D) virtual memory\n\n**13.** In a direct-mapped cache, a block can be placed in\nA) any line  B) exactly one line  C) one of two lines  D) none\n\n**14.** The average memory access time equals\nA) hit time + miss rate × miss penalty  B) hit time × miss rate  C) miss penalty / hit time  D) miss rate + miss penalty\n\n**15.** In a write-back cache a \"dirty\" bit indicates that\nA) the block has been modified and must be written to memory on eviction  B) the block is invalid  C) the block is shared  D) the block is read-only\n\n**16.** With 4 KB pages, the page offset is how many bits?\nA) 8  B) 10  C) 12  D) 16\n\n**17.** A TLB caches\nA) instructions  B) virtual-to-physical page translations  C) disk blocks  D) branch outcomes\n\n**18.** DMA allows a device to\nA) transfer data to or from memory without CPU involvement for each word  B) execute instructions  C) replace the cache  D) increase the clock\n\n**19.** In a little-endian system the least significant byte of a word is stored at\nA) the highest address  B) the lowest address  C) address zero only  D) a random address\n\n**20.** The exponent bias in IEEE 754 single precision is\nA) 15  B) 127  C) 255  D) 1023\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (10 marks)** A program has 40 percent ALU instructions (CPI 1), 30 percent loads (CPI 2), 10 percent stores (CPI 2) and 20 percent branches (CPI 2). (a) Find the average CPI [3]. (b) At 1 GHz how long do 5 × 10^8 instructions take? [2] (c) If a new design halves the CPI of loads (to 1), find the new average CPI and the speedup [3]. (d) State the formula for Amdahl's law and apply it: a part used 50 percent of the time is made 4 times faster [2].\n\n**B2. (10 marks)** Trace the program and give the final values; then encode the first instruction:\n```\nli   x5, 10\nli   x6, 3\nsub  x7, x5, x6\nslli x8, x7, 2\nadd  x9, x8, x5\n```\n(a) Give x7, x8 and x9 [4]. (b) Encode `addi x5, x0, 10` as a 32-bit hex number (li is a pseudo-instruction for it) [3]. (c) How many instructions would a `lui`-based sequence need to load 0x12345678 into a register, and which are they? [3]\n\n**B3. (10 marks)** Consider `add x1, x2, x3; sub x4, x1, x5; or x6, x4, x1; sw x6, 0(x7)`. (a) List the data dependences [3]. (b) Give the number of cycles in the pipeline (i) ideally, (ii) with forwarding, (iii) without forwarding (register file written in the first half and read in the second half of a cycle) [5]. (c) Explain why a load-use stall remains even with forwarding [2].\n\n**B4. (10 marks)** (a) A direct-mapped cache with 4 blocks of 4 bytes is accessed with the word addresses (in units of blocks) 0, 8, 0, 6, 8. Give the hit/miss sequence [3]. (b) Repeat for a 2-way set-associative cache with LRU (2 sets) [3]. (c) A 32-bit address, 8 KB direct-mapped cache with 32-byte blocks: give the offset, index and tag widths [2]. (d) Find the AMAT for a hit time of 1 ns, a miss rate of 4 percent and a miss penalty of 60 ns [2].\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **A** · 2 **B** · 3 **B** · 4 **C** · 5 **B** · 6 **C** · 7 **B** · 8 **A** · 9 **B** · 10 **B** · 11 **C** · 12 **B** · 13 **B** · 14 **A** · 15 **A** · 16 **C** · 17 **B** · 18 **A** · 19 **B** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons: 6. 00000101 inverted is 11111010, plus 1 is 11111011. 10. The loaded value is available only after MEM. 16. 4 KB = 2^12. 19. Little-endian puts the least significant byte at the lowest address.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) Step 1: CPI = Σ (fraction × CPI) = 0.4(1) + 0.3(2) + 0.1(2) + 0.2(2).\nStep 2: = 0.4 + 0.6 + 0.2 + 0.4 = **1.6**.\n(b) Time = IC × CPI/f = 5 × 10^8 × 1.6/10^9 = **0.8 s**.\n(c) Step 1: new CPI = 0.4(1) + 0.3(1) + 0.1(2) + 0.2(2) = 0.4 + 0.3 + 0.2 + 0.4 = **1.3**.\nStep 2: speedup = old time/new time = 1.6/1.3 = **1.23**.\n(d) Amdahl's law: speedup = 1/((1 - F) + F/S). With F = 0.5 and S = 4: 1/(0.5 + 0.5/4) = 1/(0.5 + 0.125) = 1/0.625 = **1.6**.\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Step 1: li x5, 10 gives x5 = 10; li x6, 3 gives x6 = 3.\nStep 2: x7 = x5 - x6 = 10 - 3 = **7**.\nStep 3: x8 = x7 << 2 = 7 × 4 = **28**.\nStep 4: x9 = x8 + x5 = 28 + 10 = **38**.\n(b) addi x5, x0, 10: imm = 10 = 0x00A (12 bits 000000001010), rs1 = 0, funct3 = 000, rd = 5, opcode 0010011.\nBits: 000000001010 00000 000 00101 0010011 = 0000 0000 1010 0000 0000 0010 1001 0011 = **0x00A00293**.\n(c) **Two instructions:** `lui x5, 0x12345` (x5 = 0x12345000) then `addi x5, x5, 0x678` (0x12345000 + 0x678 = 0x12345678; the low part 0x678 is below 0x800, so no sign-extension correction is needed).\n\n**B3.** Code: add x1,x2,x3; sub x4,x1,x5; or x6,x4,x1; sw x6,0(x7).\n(a) sub reads x1 written by add (RAW); or reads x4 written by sub (RAW) and x1 written by add (RAW, two instructions back); sw reads x6 written by or (RAW).\n(b) (i) Ideal: 5 + (4 - 1) = **8 cycles**.\n(ii) With forwarding: all producers are ALU instructions, so every result is forwarded from EX/MEM or MEM/WB with no stall: **8 cycles**.\n(iii) Without forwarding: a consumer directly after its producer waits 2 cycles for the producer's write-back. sub waits 2 cycles for add; or waits 2 cycles for sub (x1 is already written by then); sw waits 2 cycles for or: 3 × 2 = 6 stalls, so 8 + 6 = **14 cycles**.\n(c) A load produces its value only at the end of the MEM stage, whereas the next instruction needs it at the start of its EX stage, which occurs one cycle earlier: forwarding cannot go backwards in time, so one bubble must be inserted.\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Direct-mapped, 4 blocks: index = block address mod 4.\nStep 1: 0 → index 0: miss. 8 → index 0: miss (evicts 0). 0 → index 0: miss (evicts 8). 6 → index 2: miss. 8 → index 0: holds 0, so miss.\nResult **M M M M M** (0 and 8 conflict in index 0).\n(b) 2-way, 2 sets: set = block address mod 2; all of 0, 8 and 6 are even, so they map to set 0 (two ways, LRU).\nStep 1: 0: miss (set holds 0). Step 2: 8: miss (set holds 0, 8). Step 3: 0: hit (0 becomes most recent, so 8 is LRU). Step 4: 6: miss, evicts the LRU block 8 (set holds 0, 6). Step 5: 8: miss, evicts the LRU block 0.\nResult **M M H M M**: 1 hit.\n(c) Step 1: 32-byte blocks: offset = log2 32 = **5 bits**.\nStep 2: lines = 8 KB/32 B = 8192/32 = 256, so index = log2 256 = **8 bits**.\nStep 3: tag = 32 - 5 - 8 = **19 bits**.\n(d) AMAT = hit time + miss rate × miss penalty = 1 + 0.04 × 60 = 1 + 2.4 = **3.4 ns**.\n"},mcq:[{"q":"CPU time equals","options":["IC × CPI × clock period","IC / CPI","CPI / f","IC + CPI"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Amdahl's law says that the overall speedup from improving part of a program is limited by","options":["the clock rate","the fraction of time not improved","the cache size","the number of registers"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In RISC-V, register x0","options":["holds the stack pointer","always reads as zero","holds the return address","is a temporary"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"RISC-V RV32I has how many general-purpose registers?","options":["8","16","32","64"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"`lw x5, 8(x2)` loads into x5","options":["the value x2 + 8","the word at memory address x2 + 8","the value 8","the address x2"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In 8-bit two's complement, -5 is","options":["10000101","11111010","11111011","00000101"],"answer":2,"explain":"00000101 inverted is 11111010, plus 1 is 11111011.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which format does `add x5, x6, x7` use?","options":["I-type","R-type","S-type","B-type"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The five stages of the classic pipeline in order are","options":["IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB","ID, IF, EX, WB, MEM","IF, EX, ID, MEM, WB","IF, ID, MEM, EX, WB"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Forwarding (bypassing) is used to","options":["predict branches","supply a result to a dependent instruction before it is written to the register file","enlarge the cache","speed up the clock"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"With forwarding, a load followed immediately by an instruction using the loaded value causes","options":["no stall","one stall cycle","two stall cycles","a pipeline flush"],"answer":1,"explain":"The loaded value is available only after MEM.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A control hazard is caused by","options":["two instructions needing the same hardware","a dependence on a register","a branch whose outcome is not yet known","a cache miss"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Accessing an array sequentially exploits","options":["temporal locality only","spatial locality","no locality","virtual memory"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a direct-mapped cache, a block can be placed in","options":["any line","exactly one line","one of two lines","none"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The average memory access time equals","options":["hit time + miss rate × miss penalty","hit time × miss rate","miss penalty / hit time","miss rate + miss penalty"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a write-back cache a \"dirty\" bit indicates that","options":["the block has been modified and must be written to memory on eviction","the block is invalid","the block is shared","the block is read-only"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"With 4 KB pages, the page offset is how many bits?","options":["8","10","12","16"],"answer":2,"explain":"4 KB = 2^12.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A TLB caches","options":["instructions","virtual-to-physical page translations","disk blocks","branch outcomes"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"DMA allows a device to","options":["transfer data to or from memory without CPU involvement for each word","execute instructions","replace the cache","increase the clock"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In a little-endian system the least significant byte of a word is stored at","options":["the highest address","the lowest address","address zero only","a random address"],"answer":1,"explain":"Little-endian puts the least significant byte at the lowest address.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The exponent bias in IEEE 754 single precision is","options":["15","127","255","1023"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which change reduces CPU time without changing the instruction count?","options":["a lower CPI","a larger program","more instructions per task","a longer clock period"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Dynamic power in CMOS is proportional to","options":["C V f","C V² f","V / f","C / V"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The hexadecimal word 0x000000FF in 32-bit two's complement is","options":["-1","255","-255","127"],"answer":1,"explain":"FF = 255 with zero upper bytes.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The RISC-V instruction `jal ra, label` does what?","options":["branches if equal","jumps to label and saves the return address in ra","loads a word","returns from a procedure"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"To return from a procedure in RISC-V one uses","options":["jal x0, ra","ret (jalr x0, 0(ra))","lw ra, 0(sp)","beq ra, x0"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The stack in RISC-V conventionally","options":["grows towards higher addresses","grows towards lower addresses","is in the register file","is read-only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In IEEE 754 single precision, the pattern 0x3F800000 is","options":["0","1.0","2.0","NaN"],"answer":1,"explain":"Exponent 127, fraction 0.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In a single-cycle processor the clock period is determined by","options":["the average instruction","the shortest instruction","the longest instruction","the number of registers"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For the instruction `sw`, the control signal MemWrite is","options":["0","1","don't care","high impedance"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"An ideal 5-stage pipeline (no hazards) completes n instructions in","options":["5n cycles","n + 4 cycles","n cycles","5 cycles"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first instruction takes 5 cycles and each later one adds one.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A RAW hazard means","options":["read after write","write after write","write after read","read after read"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A branch predictor that uses a 2-bit saturating counter","options":["changes its prediction after one wrong outcome","needs two wrong outcomes in a row to change a strong prediction","never mispredicts","predicts always taken"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Associativity reduces mainly which kind of miss?","options":["compulsory","conflict","capacity","coherence"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For a fixed cache capacity, doubling the block size (direct-mapped) leads to","options":["more index bits","fewer index bits and more offset bits","more tag bits","no change"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A write-through cache with a write buffer","options":["never writes to memory","writes every store to memory (through the buffer)","uses dirty bits","cannot be used with DMA"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A page fault occurs when","options":["the TLB hits","the required page is not in physical memory","the cache misses","the CPU is idle"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"With 32-bit virtual addresses and 4 KB pages the number of virtual pages is","options":["2^12","2^20","2^32","2^10"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Polling I/O differs from interrupt-driven I/O in that polling","options":["is initiated by the device","is initiated by the CPU repeatedly checking a status register","uses DMA","needs no CPU"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"If a program is 10 percent serial, the maximum speedup on any number of cores is","options":["5","10","100","unlimited"],"answer":1,"explain":"1/0.1 = 10.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The MESI protocol is used to","options":["predict branches","keep the caches of several cores coherent","translate addresses","schedule instructions"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
